    {
      "question": "e-13184",
      "field": null,
      "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
      "old_sha256": "082fa1486a60848d9da5d8ade8d586c06bde1aa8dd93c5491cceac394d91aaed",
      "new_sha256": "5cb6269c38c6c9af447503b3b252d28253e6d00ed2178b476f40cca54e4b6574",
      "category": "ma_capture",
      "authoring": [],
      "source": [
        {
          "question": "e-13184",
          "field": null,
          "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
          "value": "Notice that the elements of our set\n\n$$\n\\ldots,\\quad -36, \\quad -6, \\quad -1, \\quad -\\dfrac1{6}, \\quad -\\dfrac1{36}, \\quad \\ldots\n$$\n\nform a two-way infinite geometric sequence with a common ratio $r=\\dfrac16{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\frac{1}{6} \\\\  & =(\\frac{-6}{-36}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{-1}{-6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(-\\frac{1}{6})}{(-1)} \\\\  & =\u22ef\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting  $a_1=-\\dfrac16,$ the $n$th term $a_n$ of our sequence for $n\\in\\mathbb{Z}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =(-\\frac{1}{6})\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{6})}^{n-1} \\\\  & =-(\\frac{1}{6})\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{6})}^{-1}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{6})}^{n} \\\\  & =-{(\\frac{1}{6})}^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can define our set using a constructive definition as follows:\n\n$$\nS = \\left\\{-\\left(\\dfrac16\\right)^{n} \\: : \\: n\\in \\mathbb{Z}\\right\\}\n$$",
          "category": "ma_capture",
          "file": "reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040040/example-13184.json",
          "file_sha256": "72d30fe7f318869c172c91967ff9aa4f36e03b275e755780e3f82939c96f026c"
        }
      ],
